so last Monday I did a story where I  discussed that I had finished up my  taxes for 2018 and that as a result of  Donald Trump's new tax plan my effective  federal tax rate dropped about 13  percent it was like 26 point something  percent for the 2017 tax year and it  dropped to 22 point something percent  for the 2018 tax year about a 13 percent  drop and my focus in the clip was that  economically speaking it didn't really  make much sense for the US economy the  responses were so all over the place  misunderstanding how taxes work  misunderstanding how economics works  misunderstanding even what I was saying  that I gathered some of the more common  themes from the responses that I just  want to address and I rarely do these  sort of point/counterpoint response type  things where I do a clip and then people  respond and I respond to the responses  but there were so many responses to the  tax cut clip that were so fundamentally  flawed that I thought it would be useful  so these are this is not every argument  made about how stupid I am  but these are some of the more common  themes that I saw let's put the first  one up on the screen this is an argument  that I saw a bunch give back the money  to government anyway the government  needs it for global warming this is the  idea which I actually mentioned in the  video which is David if you think your  tax rate should be higher just send in  more money to the IRS you can always do  that you can always send in more money  if you want it makes no difference  whether I choose to pay more this is not  about the amount of taxation I believe  is right for me to pay this is about the  fact that economically speaking it  doesn't make sense to give me on the  basis of having passed through income  and this deduction that Trump put in  place it's not economically logical to  give me a 13 percent tax cut that's what  I'm saying if I just write a check to  the IRS yeah they'll take the money but  the money isn't budgeted to be used in  any kind of stimulative way you can't  run a country on the idea that the  people who think they should pay more  will send more money in because  you might want to it's ridiculous I  mentioned it in the video it's just not  economic policy okay that's number one  can't even believe I have to address  that it was in the video I said in the  video if you're gonna say David pay more  here's why it doesn't make sense okay  another theme that I saw in terms of  responses people talking about me  complaining about my taxes going down  for example Tim Brock 1000 on YouTube  saying behold the first guy to ever  complain about having a lower tax  invoice totally misses the point of the  segment I'm not complaining about paying  less in taxes what I'm saying is that if  I put aside what puts more or less money  in my pocket and I explain what's good  for the economy the economy is not  better off because certain business  income has been taxed at a lower rate or  there's a deduction for a portion of  business income the whole point is it  doesn't matter whether I like having  more money or not that that's exactly  the opposite of what the segment was  about it was about the economic impact  and why I would get a 13 percent tax cut  this got very quickly into another theme  about earning the money versus earning  the tax cut okay a lot of people  commented things like for example what  did I do to earn the money I earned  great question David this is straight  out of the libertarian playbook okay in  the segment I said what have I done to  earn this tax cut okay I got hundreds of  responses from people saying David you  earned the money by working this is a  total semantic game I get that I earned  the money by working the question is  what did someone in my specific  situation do to deserve getting a tax  cut versus people in other situations  getting tax increases or decreases once  again the responses are all about me  specifically it's an illustration of how  economic policy works and I'm pointing  out that it doesn't make economic sense  to cut the taxes of people in the  particular business owning situation in  which I find myself okay another theme  David just spend the money it'll be good  for the economy one example you do  realize that you can spend the money  that you earned right and spending this  money helps the economy it's okay  to spend your money this is the whole  point okay  people with businesses who aren't  incorporating that tax cut into their  personal spending month-to-month are not  that likely to spend the money I did the  same thing I always do with my tax  refund I saved it this isn't a hugely  economically stimulative thing to do I  wasn't counting on that money i budgeted  for the business and for everything for  the year on the basis of not getting the  cut I don't buy random things when I get  a little extra money I save the money  and this is exactly why it is not a good  way to stimulate the economy and people  who are actually wealthy do you think  that they go and spend that money on  stuff in the traditionally economically  stimulative way no they don't  this is why this type of pass-through  business income tax cut doesn't make  sense and then lastly the charity  argument why don't you give your extra  money to charities that's perfectly fine  it does not end up being very  economically stimulative it does not  justify tax cuts for the rich the  question isn't what can David Pakman  come up with to do with money that  results from a lower tax rate at the end  of the year the question is what should  government policy be around how to best  tax and spend government revenues me  giving money to charity is fine but it's  not policy it's at my discretion only  part of that charity donation even goes  to doing the good that charities do  there's overhead and all sorts of other  stuff so it really doesn't make sense  and then the last one which we don't  have a screenshot of is David you should  just reinvest the money into the  business to grow the business  exponentially  stop people saying that online you don't  grow a business exponentially with a  single tax cut which is likely not even  going to be permanent since much of what  has been done by the new tax plan is  temporary anyway it would be really bad  planning to on the basis of a tax cut  one year start doing whatever it is that  these people imagine would be done with  the money so just a lot of nonsense  responses from people who just don't  really seem to understand what the point  is of pointing out at a policy level  what doesn't doesn't make sense rather  than can david pakman donate the money  to charity that's not really the issue  here
 

